N('salt-lake-tabernacle','Salt Lake Tabernacle','Salt Lake City','ut','worship','standing',40.77042,-111.89314,'exact','open','walk','','1864–1867',
  "The long domed meeting hall on Temple Square, built in 1864 to 1867 on 44 sandstone piers designed by William H. Folsom, with a lattice-truss roof raised under Henry Grow; Truman O. Angell later worked on its acoustics. It held the church's general conference until 2000 and is the home of the Tabernacle Choir. Its pipe organ, at the west end over the choir seats, was rebuilt many times; the present instrument was finished in 1948 and has 11,623 pipes. The building was closed from 2005 to 2007 for seismic work.",
  'The oval dome on its ring of sandstone piers, and inside the gilded front pipes of the organ. The original benches were replaced with oak pews in 2007.',
  '','On Temple Square, owned by The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. The choir\'s broadcast, Music & the Spoken Word, is open to the public each Sunday morning; the church\'s Temple Square page lists the season\'s events.',
  [W('Salt Lake Tabernacle'),TS,NR],arch='Henry Grow; William H. Folsom; Truman O. Angell',archw='Wikipedia: Henry Grow oversaw the building, Folsom designed the piers, Angell added the 1870 gallery',
  brick='?',hours='Music & the Spoken Word, Sundays 9:30 a.m.; other hours on the Temple Square page'),
N('salt-lake-temple','Salt Lake Temple','Salt Lake City','ut','worship','standing',40.77056,-111.89194,'exact','view','walk','','1853–1893',
  "The granite temple at the heart of Temple Square, designed by Truman O. Angell. Its cornerstone was laid on April 6, 1853 and it was dedicated exactly forty years later; the walls are quartz monzonite from Little Cottonwood Canyon, about 20 miles away, over a partly sandstone foundation that was buried during the Utah War. It closed at the end of 2019 for seismic retrofitting, and the end of that work was announced on November 23, 2026.",
  'The granite walls and spires, the statue of the angel Moroni on its capstone (put back on April 2, 2024), and the symbols carved on the walls, seen from the grounds of Temple Square.',
  '','Owned by The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. The grounds of Temple Square are open to all; the temple itself is entered only by church members once dedicated. The church\'s page says a public open house, with tickets, runs from April 5 to October 1, 2027.',
  [W('Salt Lake Temple'),TS,NR],arch='Truman O. Angell',archw='Wikipedia names Angell as the architect',brick='no',bcol='none'),
N('cathedral-of-the-madeleine-slc','Cathedral of the Madeleine','Salt Lake City','ut','worship','standing',40.76969,-111.88172,'exact','open','walk','','1900–1909',
  "The Catholic cathedral of the Diocese of Salt Lake City, built in 1900 to 1909 to designs by Carl M. Neuhausen and Bernard O. Mecklenburg, Romanesque outside and Gothic inside. In 1915 to 1917 Bishop Joseph S. Glass had John T. Comès decorate the interior, with shrines, woodwork by Johannes Kirchmayer and murals by Felix Lieftuchter. The sandstone front was restored in 1975 and the inside cleaned and restored for its 1993 rededication.",
  'The Utah sandstone front; inside, the painted murals, carved woodwork and stained glass of 1915 to 1917.',
  '','331 East South Temple; a working parish. The parish\'s page says the cathedral is open every day from 7 a.m. to 8:30 p.m. and gives guided tours on some days.',
  [W('Cathedral of the Madeleine'),['The cathedral\'s page','https://www.utcotm.org/'],NR],arch='Carl M. Neuhausen; Bernard O. Mecklenburg',archw='Wikipedia: the bishop hired Neuhausen and Mecklenburg; John T. Comès did the interior in 1915',
  brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Open daily 7 a.m.–8:30 p.m.; Sunday Mass 8:30, 11, 3 (Spanish); Saturday 5 and 7 p.m. (Spanish); daily 8 a.m., Mon–Fri 5:15 p.m.'),
N('slc-and-county-building','Salt Lake City and County Building','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.75962,-111.88680,'exact','open','walk','','1891–1894',
  "The city hall of Salt Lake City, a Richardsonian Romanesque pile of gray Kyune sandstone with a central clock tower, designed by Monheim, Bird and Proudfoot and dedicated on December 28, 1894. It cost nearly $900,000, more than twice the bid. It also held the county offices, the first public library, and the courtroom where Joe Hill was sentenced in 1914. Statues missing from its roof were replaced when it was restored in 1989 and set on base isolators against earthquakes; it reopened to the public in November 2021 after a closure.",
  'Carved sandstone on every face, with carvings of pioneer women above the granite columns on the east and west sides.',
  '','451 South State Street, in Washington Square; city offices inside keep office hours (the business licensing page gives Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.).',
  [W('Salt Lake City and County Building'),['Salt Lake City: business licensing (office hours)','https://www.slc.gov/Finance/business-licensing/contact-business-licensing/'],NR],
  arch='Monheim, Bird and Proudfoot',archw='Wikipedia names the firm, formed in 1891 to design the building',brick='no',bcol='none',hours='City offices Mon–Fri 8 a.m.–5 p.m.'),
N('boston-and-newhouse-buildings','Boston and Newhouse Buildings','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.76108,-111.89070,'site','view','walk','','1908–1911',
  "Two eleven-story office towers facing each other across Exchange Place, designed by Henry Ives Cobb of Chicago and counted as the city's first skyscrapers. The mining magnate Samuel Newhouse built them for a new business district meant as a western Wall Street; two more towers planned for the far end of the street were never built. Both are steel-framed and faced with Indiana limestone: the Boston has Ionic columns and the Newhouse two-story arches and Doric columns. Exchange Place between them was closed to cars in 1979 and made a brick-paved plaza.",
  'Banded limestone, a row of cartouches and lion heads on the Boston Building, copper-plated trim on the Newhouse. Old street lamps, mosaic tiles in the sidewalk and brass railings down to the basements survive. Shops on the lower floors and offices above.',
  '','9 and 10 Exchange Place, off Main Street between 300 and 400 South; the plaza between them is open to walk through. Offices inside are private.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Boston and Newhouse Buildings','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/82942'],W('Exchange Place Historic District'),NR],
  arch='Henry Ives Cobb',archw='SAH Archipedia: designed by Chicago architect Henry Ives Cobb',brick='no',bcol='none'),
N('hotel-utah-slc','Hotel Utah (Joseph Smith Memorial Building)','Salt Lake City','ut','hotel','standing',40.76968,-111.89045,'exact','open','walk','','1909–1911',
  "A ten-story hotel in the Second Renaissance Revival style, designed by Parkinson and Bergstrom of Los Angeles and opened on June 9, 1911, on the corner of Main Street and South Temple. The church was its main stockholder. It admitted no Black guests: Marian Anderson was allowed to stay only if she kept off the elevator and ate in her room. It closed as a hotel in August 1987 and was remade for church and community use by 1993, as the Joseph Smith Memorial Building; it closed again in 2023 for renovation.",
  'White glazed terra cotta and brick over a frame of concrete and steel.',
  '','15 East South Temple, owned by The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. The church\'s Temple Square page lists exhibits for families in the building again; days and times were not read.',
  [W('Joseph Smith Memorial Building'),TS,NR],arch='Parkinson and Bergstrom',archw='Wikipedia names the Los Angeles firm',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tribune-building-slc','Tribune Building','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.76606,-111.89083,'site','view','walk','','1924',
  "A commercial high-rise of 1924 by Pope and Burton, one of only four built in the city between the First World War and the Depression. It is also called the Ezra Thompson Building, after the three-time mayor, and was the home of the Salt Lake Tribune until 2005; Neumont University moved in in 2013. The street front has been changed, but the terra cotta cornice is still there.",
  'Look up: the terra cotta cornice at the top is the original work.',
  '','143 South Main Street. A college building; look from the street.',
  [W('Tribune Building (Salt Lake City)'),NR],arch='Pope and Burton',archw='Wikipedia: a work of architects Pope & Burton',brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('walker-bank-building-slc','Walker Bank Building','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.76530,-111.89060,'exact','view','walk','','1911–1912',
  "An office tower of the Chicago school built for the Walker brothers' bank by Eames and Young of St. Louis, opened on December 9, 1912. The bank had its vault in the basement and its hall on the main floor, with offices above. On top is a 64-foot weather tower whose lights give the forecast: put up by a radio station in the 1940s, taken down in the 1980s and put back in 2008.",
  'The weather beacon on the roof.',
  '','175 South Main Street (the Walker Center). Private offices; look from the street.',
  [W('Walker Center'),NR],arch='Eames and Young',archw='Wikipedia names the St. Louis firm',brick='?'),
N('first-presbyterian-church-slc','First Presbyterian Church','Salt Lake City','ut','worship','standing',40.76976,-111.88023,'site','open','walk','','1903–1905',
  "A Gothic Revival church of red sandstone from Red Butte Canyon, built in 1903 to 1905 to designs by Walter E. Ware and patterned on Carlisle Cathedral in England. The congregation met from 1874 in an earlier church at Second South and Second East, since torn down. Its first organ, by the Bennett Organ Company, was opened with a concert by Clarence Eddy in 1911.",
  'Red sandstone walls and tower at the corner of South Temple and C Street; interior not checked.',
  '','371 East South Temple (the church gives its address as 12 C Street). An active congregation; the church\'s page gives one Sunday service and an interior ramp to the sanctuary.',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church of Salt Lake City'),['The church\'s page for visitors','https://www.fpcslc.org/about-us'],NR],
  arch='Walter E. Ware',archw='Wikipedia: designed by architect Walter E. Ware',brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Sunday worship 10 a.m.'),
N('salt-lake-masonic-temple','Salt Lake Masonic Temple','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.76905,-111.87229,'exact','open','walk','','1925–1927',
  "The headquarters of the Grand Lodge of Utah, finished in 1927 to designs by Carl W. Scott and counted the city's best example of Egyptian Revival. The Egyptian manner was chosen so the building would not look like other Masonic temples, and it is faced with a brick made for it, called Temple Brick, that later became popular. It has been in use without a break since it opened, run by the Salt Lake Masonic Temple Association.",
  'An Egyptian front on South Temple. Inside, ceremonial rooms in Egyptian, Colonial, Venetian, Gothic, Moorish and Tudor styles, seen on a guided tour.',
  '','650 East South Temple. Preservation Utah\'s volunteers lead ticketed tours of the inside on set evenings; see its tour page for dates.',
  [W('Salt Lake Masonic Temple'),['Preservation Utah: Masonic Temple tour','https://www.preservationutah.org/events-1/salt-lake-masonic-temple-tour'],NR],
  arch='Carl W. Scott',archw='Wikipedia infobox names Carl W. Scott',brick='?',hours='Guided tours on set dates, by ticket from Preservation Utah'),
N('slc-union-pacific-depot','Salt Lake City Union Pacific Depot','Salt Lake City','ut','rail','standing',40.76905,-111.90298,'exact','open','walk','stations','1908–1909',
  "Union Station, built in 1908 and 1909 for the San Pedro, Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad and the Oregon Short Line, later both Union Pacific, under D. J. Patterson, the Southern Pacific's architect. A sandstone building in the French Second Empire style with a terrazzo floor and stained glass. On October 31, 2024 it reopened as the lobby, restaurants and suites of a hotel, the Asher Adams, with a new building behind it.",
  'Second Empire roofs over sandstone; inside, the stained glass, gold leaf and tile kept in the hotel\'s restoration.',
  '','12 South 400 West. A working hotel, owned and run privately; its restaurants and lobby are in the old station.',
  [W('Salt Lake City Union Pacific Depot'),NR],arch='D. J. Patterson; John Dove Isaacs',archw='Wikipedia infobox: D. J. Patterson and John Dove Isaacs',brick='no',bcol='none',hours='?'),
N('drgw-depot-slc','Denver and Rio Grande Western Depot','Salt Lake City','ut','rail','standing',40.76281,-111.90448,'exact','view','walk','stations','1910',
  "The Rio Grande's station, built in 1910 for $750,000 to designs by the Chicago architect Henry Schlacks, in Renaissance Revival and Beaux-Arts styles, and meant to outdo the Union Pacific station built a year earlier. It was bought for one dollar in 1977 and it became the home of the Utah State Historical Society. It was damaged by the magnitude 5.7 earthquake of March 2020; the collections were moved out, and the state says the depot will be an office of its history division again once it reopens.",
  'The long station front at the end of 300 South.',
  '','300 South Rio Grande Street, owned by the State of Utah. Not open to the public as of KSL\'s 2023 report; look from the street.',
  [W('Denver and Rio Grande Western Depot (Salt Lake City)'),['KSL: research center reopens elsewhere (2023)','https://www.ksl.com/article/news/features/historic/want-to-view-utah-history-state-reopens-research-center-after-nearly-3-years/50567095'],NR],
  arch='Henry Schlacks',archw='Wikipedia: designed by Chicago architect Henry Schlacks',brick='?'),
N('west-high-school-slc','West High School','Salt Lake City','ut','civic','standing',40.77478,-111.90046,'exact','view','walk','','1922',
  "Utah's oldest high school has been housed since 1922 in this three-story building on Union Square, put up after the old University of Utah main building on the square was torn down in 1921. A new building across the street was begun in March 2026; what is planned for the old one was not found.",
  'The 1922 school on Union Square.',
  '','241 North 300 West. A working public school; look from the street.',
  [W('West High School (Utah)')],brick='?'),
N('saltair-resort-site','Saltair (the resort site and pilings)','Magna','ut','amuse','traces',40.74713,-112.18804,'area','view','drive','amusements,burned','1893–1970',
  "A resort on the south shore of the Great Salt Lake. The first Saltair, a Moorish Revival pavilion by Richard K. A. Kletting, stood on more than 2,000 posts and pilings over the water and burned on April 22, 1925. A second pavilion with the largest dance floor of its day was built in its place, but the lake drew back in 1933 and fires in 1931, 1967 and 1970 ended it. A third, smaller Saltair was built in 1981, flooded within months, and since 2005 has been a concert hall.",
  'Rows of the old pilings still run out toward the lake from the shore, the remains of the trestle and pier to the first Saltair. The 1981 hall stands nearby.',
  '','12408 West Saltair Drive, off Interstate 80 west of the city. The 1981 building is a privately owned concert venue open only for shows. Where the pilings can best be seen from public ground was not checked.',
  [W('Saltair (Utah)'),['The Saltair (concert venue)','https://thesaltair.com/']],arch='Richard K. A. Kletting',archw='Wikipedia: the first resort was designed by Richard K. A. Kletting',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
